---
title: Return of the $10 Server
---

A few years back I was tooling around ebay looking for a deal on a RasPi when I
absentmindedly placed a $12 bid on an 2009 onyx black HP G60-230US laptop and
immediately forgot about it until a week later when the piper came a'piping.

It didn't have a battery or a power adapter, but that was fine because I
just-so-happened to have another (bricked) HP laptop with the same exact
battery and charger! So I made good and set to work distrohopping; it has a
real nice keyboard and in 2020 it wasn't a bad old computer, Pentium T4200
(dual core) @ 2.000GHz, Intel Mobile 4 Series Chipset "GPU", but it always
needed a bit of finagling with the wifi. Eventually I landed on Alpine Linux,
because I wanted to see what it could do with a lightweight distro, fixed the
wifi once. Played a bit with [FVWM](https://fvwm.org) but decided I had enough
desktop environments and so ended up setting up [Docker,](https://docker.com),
configured it so I could leave it running with the lid closed, and installed a
bunch of little projects with an external USB drive as storage --a brief learning
exercise-- and every now and then I'd open it up on a shelf behind my desk and
run `cmatrix` in the background behind me on stream. Then one day there was a
thunderstorm, and it didn't come back on ever again --or so I thought.

A few months ago I got around finally checking on it in earnest, and nothing.
Screen made a sad red flash, then the wifi light turned from blue to red and it
just sat there, dug out a VGA monitor and tried that, still nothing, and so it
sat wedged behind that monitor for a few more months. Well, this week, I got it
out again and as it was booting I mashed the keyboard and noticed the sad red
flash of the screen had hints of HP logo, maybe some bootloader: so I watched
and waited, the disk started spinning, happily blinking away, but seemingly
doing nothing else. Until I used the Computer equivalent of blowing in the NES
Cartridge and slid my finger up and down the F1-F12 keys like a rock'n'roll
pianist. Another flash, and the sad red wifi light turned blue -- it had
connected to the local network! Boom! and I'm suddenly back in business!

SSH'd right in and now I have my fun little home server again; We learned it's
not powerful enough to let 2 people play Minecraft together, but it's plenty
powerful to run YaCy search engine, Minflux RSS reader, Jellyfin, and a few
other docker containers and idle around 2% CPU; or when hitting all the
end-points it was hovering around 50% CPU usage with a few higher spikes. It
seems all well and good again; nothing lost on the hard-drive; so I setup a new
backup regiment (a few lines of rsync commands) and added a git account to try
to arrange some project I've got floating in limbo; and we'll see what I end up
using it for next!

